; Iris (Svelte/Vite SPA). Vite keeps its own incremental cache in-tree under
; node_modules/, so we run the build in the real source tree (outside dune's
; _build/ sandbox) and then copy the static assets into the `ui-export`
; directory target. It is deliberately NOT named `dist`: Vite already writes an
; in-tree dist/, and a target sharing that name conflicts with the source
; directory on any machine that has built before.
;
; The export MUST be a real dune target, not just the build.tag stamp. Tracked
; only by build.tag, a restored _build/ (from the Docker /dune-cache mount, or
; any dune cache hit) with an up-to-date stamp made the rule look satisfied while
; code/src/iris/dist/ did not exist -- dist/ is gitignored, so it is never in a
; clean checkout / Docker build context -- so `dune build @code/src/iris/all`
; silently skipped Vite and the image's UI COPY found nothing. Making the export
; a directory target means it travels inside _build/ and survives the cache
; restore. docker/stategraph/Dockerfile documents the same trap; hermes solves it the
; same way for its Next.js export (ui-export).

(rule
 (targets
  build.tag
  (dir ui-export))
 (deps
  package.json
  package-lock.json
  index.html
  vite.config.js
  svelte.config.js
  tailwind.config.js
  postcss.config.js
  tsconfig.json
  knip.json
  ../../../api_schemas/terrat/api.json
  (glob_files_rec src/*)
  (glob_files_rec scripts/*)
  (glob_files_rec public/*))
 (action
  (progn
   (bash
    "cd $DUNE_SOURCEROOT/code/src/iris && (test -L api.json || ln -sf ../../../api_schemas/terrat/api.json api.json) && for i in 1 2 3; do npm install && break || { echo \"npm install failed (try $i/3), sleeping 15s...\"; sleep 15; }; done && npm run generate-api-types && npm run build")
   ; dune does not pre-create directory targets, so make it before copying in.
   (bash
    "rm -rf ui-export && mkdir -p ui-export && cp -R $DUNE_SOURCEROOT/code/src/iris/dist/. ui-export/")
   (bash "touch build.tag"))))

(alias
 (name all)
 (deps build.tag))
